<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    ============================================================================
    THIS PROJECT HAS NO <ProjectReference> AND NO THIRD-PARTY <PackageReference>.
    THAT IS THE POINT. IT IS NOT AN OVERSIGHT.
    ============================================================================

    The Domain layer is the centre of Clean Architecture. Every arrow points
    inward, towards here, and nothing points out. Domain knows nothing about:

      - Entity Framework, SQL Server, or any database
      - ASP.NET Core, HTTP, controllers, or status codes
      - JSON, Swagger, or any serialization format
      - FluentValidation, Mapster, or any other library

    Try it yourself, then undo it. Run this from the repo root:

        dotnet add src/HelpDisk.Domain package Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore

    It will succeed - NuGet has no opinion about your architecture. Nothing
    enforces this rule except the people reading the code. That is exactly why
    it is written down here, at the one place where somebody would break it.

    Why it matters: business rules that depend on no framework can be tested
    with no mocks, survive a rewrite of the database or the web framework, and
    stay readable to a domain expert who does not write C#.
  -->

</Project>
